---
import Page from '../../layouts/Page.astro';
---

<Page path="start/install">
<section id="binary">
  <h2 id="binary"><a href="#binary">Ready-made binaries</a></h2>
    <div role="tablist" aria-label="Install method">
    <button role="tab" id="t-curl" aria-selected="true" aria-controls="p-curl" type="button">curl | sh</button>
    <button role="tab" id="t-brew" aria-selected="false" aria-controls="p-brew" tabindex="-1" type="button">Homebrew</button>
    <button role="tab" id="t-mise" aria-selected="false" aria-controls="p-mise" tabindex="-1" type="button">mise</button>
    <button role="tab" id="t-deb" aria-selected="false" aria-controls="p-deb" tabindex="-1" type="button">.deb</button>
  </div>
  <div role="tabpanel" id="p-curl" aria-labelledby="t-curl" tabindex="0"><div class="code"><pre><span class="p">$ </span>curl -fsSL https://multiversectl.dev/install.sh | sh
<span class="c"># linux/darwin, amd64/arm64; set MULTIVERSECTL_VERSION to pin a release</span></pre></div></div>
  <div role="tabpanel" id="p-brew" aria-labelledby="t-brew" tabindex="0" hidden><div class="code"><pre><span class="p">$ </span>brew install cristiansteib/tap/multiversectl</pre></div><p class="muted">Or <code>brew tap cristiansteib/tap</code> once, then <code>brew install multiversectl</code>.</p></div>
  <div role="tabpanel" id="p-mise" aria-labelledby="t-mise" tabindex="0" hidden><div class="code"><pre><span class="p">$ </span>mise use -g ubi:cristiansteib/multiversectl</pre></div></div>
  <div role="tabpanel" id="p-deb" aria-labelledby="t-deb" tabindex="0" hidden><p class="muted">Download the <code>.deb</code> from the <a href="https://github.com/cristiansteib/multiversectl/releases">releases page</a>, then:</p><div class="code"><pre><span class="p">$ </span>sudo dpkg -i multiversectl_*.deb</pre></div></div>
  <p>The script, Homebrew and the <code>.deb</code> also install the alias <code>mvctl</code> and the <a href="../tasks/server.html">web view</a>, <code>multiversectl-server</code>, beside the command. <code>mise</code> installs the command line only, so <code>multiversectl server</code> then needs the other program on the <code>PATH</code>. <code>multiversectl self-update</code> replaces both with the latest release.</p>
</section>

<section id="build">
  <h2 id="build"><a href="#build">From source</a></h2>
  <p>Go 1.27 or newer:</p>
  <div class="code"><pre><span class="p">$ </span>git clone https://github.com/cristiansteib/multiversectl
<span class="p">$ </span>cd multiversectl &amp;&amp; make install   <span class="c"># builds into ~/.local/bin</span></pre></div>
  <p><code>make install</code> honours <code>PREFIX</code>, so <code>make install PREFIX=/usr/local/bin</code> puts it on the system path instead.</p>
</section>

<section id="remove">
  <h2 id="remove"><a href="#remove">Remove it, completely</a></h2>
  <div class="code"><pre><span class="p">$ </span>multiversectl system footprint</pre></div>
  <p>It lists everything multiversectl has put on the machine — the server and proxy if running, every universe with its containers, volumes and worktrees, the clones, branches left behind, the configuration, caches, completions and the programs — and removes none of it. Each item has its size and its removal command, and anything that would lose work existing nowhere else is marked. The commands come last, in a safe order; <code>--script</code> prints only those.</p>
  <p>Run it before <code>system reset</code>: once a multiverse is forgotten the tool no longer knows its folder. The root catalog's repository is not touched.</p>
</section>

<section id="verify">
  <h2 id="verify"><a href="#verify">Checking what you downloaded</a></h2>
  <p>Every release publishes <code>checksums.txt</code>, and <code>self-update</code> verifies each file against it before replacing anything: a download that does not match is not installed, and a release without the list is not installed from either.</p>
  <p>The list itself is signed, with a short-lived certificate tied to the workflow that built the release rather than to a key anyone could take, so you can check it yourself:</p>
  <div class="code"><pre><span class="p">$ </span>cosign verify-blob checksums.txt \
    --signature checksums.txt.sig --certificate checksums.txt.pem \
    --certificate-identity-regexp <span class="s">'^https://github.com/cristiansteib/multiversectl/'</span> \
    --certificate-oidc-issuer https://token.actions.githubusercontent.com
<span class="p">$ </span>sha256sum -c checksums.txt --ignore-missing</pre></div>
</section>

<section id="completion">
  <h2 id="completion"><a href="#completion">Shell completions</a></h2>
  <div class="code"><pre><span class="p">$ </span>multiversectl completion</pre></div>
  <p>It finds your shell from <code>$SHELL</code>, says which files it will write, and asks. <code>--yes</code> answers for you, which is what a setup script wants. Everything goes under your home directory; nothing needs root.</p>
  <ul class="t">
    <li><strong>bash</strong>: <code>~/.local/share/bash-completion/completions/multiversectl</code>, loaded on demand by the bash-completion package.</li>
    <li><strong>fish</strong>: <code>~/.config/fish/completions/multiversectl.fish</code>, which fish loads by itself.</li>
    <li><strong>zsh</strong>: <code>~/.local/share/multiversectl/completions/multiversectl.zsh</code>, plus a block between two marker comments in <code>~/.zshrc</code> that sources it. Nothing puts a user directory on zsh's <code>$fpath</code>, so the startup file has to.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>The installed file records the version that wrote it, and after any upgrade the next command rewrites it, so completions follow the binary. It only updates a script already there. <code>completion --uninstall</code> removes the file and the block.</p>
</section>

<section id="needs">
  <h2 id="needs"><a href="#needs">What it needs on the machine</a></h2>
  <ul class="t">
    <li>A container runtime with <code>docker compose</code> v2. On macOS that is Docker Desktop, OrbStack or Colima; on Linux, Docker Engine or Podman.</li>
    <li><code>git</code>, new enough for <code>git worktree</code>.</li>
    <li>Nothing else: it runs no daemon of its own, and the only long-lived process it starts is the shared proxy, which is a container.</li>
  </ul>
  <p><code>multiversectl universe doctor</code> checks all of it and names what is missing.</p>
  <p>Releases are built for Linux and macOS on <code>amd64</code> and <code>arm64</code>. The script installs into <code>~/.local/bin</code>, which macOS does not put on the <code>PATH</code> by default, and warns when it is missing. Completions work for zsh and fish; macOS ships bash 3.2 with no <code>bash-completion</code> to load them.</p>
</section>
</Page>
